=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies in the BIND 9 DNS server (`named`) running on **srv1** (10.5.0.4), the campus DNS server referenced in PC1's `/etc/resolv.conf`. The configuration file `/etc/bind/named.conf` — written when the lab started on 2026-08-04 — contains the directive `recursion no;` in its global options block. BIND is therefore operating in pure authoritative mode: it answers only for the single zone it owns (`jeremysitlab.com`) and returns an immediate `REFUSED` response to every recursive query for any external domain name (e.g., `google.com`). This was confirmed by `nslookup google.com 10.5.0.4` → **REFUSED**, while `nslookup srv1.jeremysitlab.com 10.5.0.4` → 10.5.0.4 succeeds normally.

All other layers of the network are healthy: PC1 reaches its default gateway (10.1.0.1), OSPF correctly distributes r1's `default-information originate` default route through csw1/csw2 down to dswa1/dswa2 and on to PC1, r1's NAT MASQUERADE rules for 10.1.0.0/24 are intact, r1 can reach ispa (203.0.113.1), and PC1 can successfully ping simulated internet addresses (e.g., 66.235.200.145, TTL=61) by IP — confirming that the data plane is fully intact. Only DNS name resolution is broken.

Because all modern web browsing begins with a DNS lookup, a `REFUSED` from the only configured resolver (10.5.0.4) silently kills every HTTP/HTTPS request before a TCP connection is even attempted, producing the "spinning / timing-out" symptom reported by the user.

**Root cause:** `recursion no;` was injected into `/etc/bind/named.conf` on srv1, converting the DNS server from a recursive resolver into an authoritative-only server.

**Fix:** On srv1, edit `/etc/bind/named.conf`, change `recursion no;` to `recursion yes;`, add `allow-recursion { 10.0.0.0/8; };` to restrict recursive access to internal clients, and restart named (`kill -HUP <pid>` or `rndc reload`).
=== END ===